Assistant:  Here's an example of how you can draw shapes using `PdfPageBuilder`. Note that `PdfColor` is used to set the color of the shapes and `PdfLineStyle` is used to set the line width.

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using UglyToad.PdfPig;
using UglyToad.PdfPig.Content;
using UglyToad.PdfPig.Core;
using UglyToad.PdfPig.Writer;

public class ShapeDrawer
{
    public static void DrawShapes()
    {
        string filePath = @"C:\Users\Kris\test.pdf";
        using (FileStream fileStream = new FileStream(filePath, FileMode.Create))
        {
            PdfDocumentBuilder builder = new PdfDocumentBuilder();

            PdfPageBuilder page = builder.AddPage(PdfPageSize.A4);

            PdfColor blueColor = new PdfRgbColor(0, 0, 255);
            PdfLineStyle lineStyle = new PdfLineStyle(blueColor, 2);

            // Draw a line
            page.AddLine(new PdfPoint(10, 10), new PdfPoint(100, 100), lineStyle);

            // Draw a rectangle
            PdfColor redColor = new PdfRgbColor(255, 0, 0);
            PdfLineStyle rectangleStyle = new PdfLineStyle(redColor, 3);
            page.AddRectangle(new PdfPoint(50, 50), 50, 50, rectangleStyle);

            PdfDocument document = builder.Build();
            document.Save(fileStream);
        }
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        DrawShapes();
    }
}
```

In this example, a line is drawn from point (10,10) to point (100,100) with a blue color and line width of 2. A red rectangle is then drawn at point (50,50) with a width and height of 50 and a line width of 3.

If you want to make the color and line width configurable, you can add parameters to the `DrawShapes` method:

```csharp
