Assistant:   Unfortunately, PdfPig doesn't include a built-in NearestNeighbourWordExtractor. However, you can use Tesseract OCR (Optical Character Recognition) library, such as Tesseract.NET, to extract words from a PDF using OCR. Here's how you can implement the `GetWordsForPage` method using Tesseract.NET:

First, you need to install Tesseract.NET via NuGet Package Manager by searching for "Tesseract".

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.IO;
using Tesseract;
using UglyToad.PdfPig;

namespace PdfPigExample
{
    public class PdfWordExtractor
    {
        public Dictionary<int, List<string>> GetWordsForPage(string pdfPath)
        {
            var wordsByPage = new Dictionary<int, List<string>>();

            using (var document = new Document(pdfPath))
            {
                for (int i = 1; i <= document.PageCount; i++)
                {
                    using (var page = document.GetPage(i))
                    {
                        var pageContent = new Image(page.GetImage());

                        using (var engine = new Engine("tesseract.exe", new Config()))
                        {
                            engine.Init();
                            engine.SetPageSegmentMode(PageSegMode.PSM_AUTO);
                            engine.SetVariable("tessedit_char_whitelist", "0123456789abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ");

                            var pageText = engine.Process(pageContent).GetText();
                            engine.End();

                            wordsByPage[i] = pageText.Split(' ').ToList();
                        }
                    }
                }
            }

            return wordsByPage;
        }
    }
}
```

This code defines a new class `PdfWordExtractor` with a `GetWordsForPage` method. The method extracts words from all pages in the specified PDF file using Tesseract OCR and returns a dictionary where the keys are page numbers and the values are lists of words for each page.

Keep in mind that OCR may not be 100% accurate, and the result might include errors, especially with handwriting or complex fonts.
