Assistant:  I'll help you create a new class called `PdfMerger` that merges one or more PDF files into a single document using the iText7 library. Here's an outline of the class and the `MergePdfFiles` method.

```csharp
using iText.Kernel.Pdf;
using System.IO;
using System.Linq;

public class PdfMerger
{
    public byte[] MergePdfFiles(params string[] inputFilePaths)
    {
        using (MemoryStream mergedPdfOutput = new MemoryStream())
        using (PdfDocument mergedDocument = new PdfDocument(mergedPdfOutput))
        {
            PdfDocument currentDocument = null;

            foreach (string inputFile in inputFilePaths)
            {
                if (currentDocument != null)
                    mergedDocument.Add(currentDocument);

                using (PdfDocument documentToMerge = new PdfDocument(new PdfReader(inputFile)))
                {
                    currentDocument = documentToMerge;
                }
            }

            mergedDocument.Close();
            currentDocument.Close();

            return mergedPdfOutput.ToArray();
        }
    }
}
```

The `MergePdfFiles` method accepts one or more input file paths as parameters and returns the merged PDF as a byte array. It merges the PDFs by iterating through each input file, adding the document to the `mergedDocument` before the current one, and then closing both documents after processing all input files.

To use this class, create an instance and call the `MergePdfFiles` method:

```csharp
