User: Create a C# class that demonstrates extracting images and hyperlinks from a page in C:\Users\Kris\test.pdf. Expose methods called GetImagesFromPage and GetHyperlinksFromPage.
Assistant:  To extract images and hyperlinks from a PDF page using C#, you can use the `UglyToad.PdfPig` library, which provides a simple and efficient way to interact with PDF files. Below is an example of how you can create a class to perform these operations.

First, ensure you have the `UglyToad.PdfPig` package installed via NuGet:

```bash
dotnet add package UglyToad.PdfPig
```

Now, create a class called `PdfExtractor` that includes methods to extract images and hyperlinks from a specified page in the PDF file.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.IO;
using UglyToad.PdfPig;
using UglyToad.PdfPig.Content;
using UglyToad.PdfPig.Images;

public class PdfExtractor
{
    public List<ExtractedImage> GetImagesFromPage(string filePath, int pageNumber)
    {
        using (PdfDocument document = PdfDocument.Open(filePath))
        {
            if (pageNumber < 1 || pageNumber > document.PageCount)
            {
                throw new ArgumentException("Page number is out of range.");
            }

            Page page = document.GetPage(pageNumber);
            List<ExtractedImage> images = new List<ExtractedImage>();

            foreach (Image image in page.Images)
            {
                images.Add(new ExtractedImage
                {
                    ImageData = image.Data,
                    Left = image.Bounds.Left,
                    Bottom = image.Bounds.Bottom,
                    Width = image.Bounds.Width,
                    Height = image.Bounds.Height
                });
            }

            return images;
        }
    }

    public List<ExtractedHyperlink> GetHyperlinksFromPage(string filePath, int pageNumber)
    {
        using (PdfDocument document = PdfDocument.Open(filePath))
        {
            if (pageNumber < 1 || pageNumber > document.PageCount)
            {
                throw new ArgumentException("Page number is out of range.");
            }

            Page page = document.GetPage(pageNumber);
            List<ExtractedHyperlink> hyperlinks = new List<ExtractedHyperlink>();

            foreach (Link link in page.Links)
            {
                hyperlinks.Add(new ExtractedHyperlink
                {
                    Text = link.Label,
                    Uri = link.Uri,
                    Left = link.Bounds.Left,
                    Bottom = link.Bounds.Bottom,
                    Width = link.Bounds.Width,
                    Height = link.Bounds.Height
                });
            }

            return hyperlinks;
        }
    }
}

public class ExtractedImage
{
    public byte[] ImageData { get; set; }
    public float Left { get; set; }
    public float Bottom { get; set; }
    public float Width { get; set; }
    public float Height { get; set; }
}

public class ExtractedHyperlink
{
    public string Text { get; set; }
    public Uri Uri { get; set; }
    public float Left { get; set; }
    public float Bottom { get; set; }
    public float Width { get; set; }
    public float Height { get; set; }
}
```

### Usage Example

You can use the `PdfExtractor` class to extract images and hyperlinks from a specific page in a PDF file.

```csharp
class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        string filePath = @"C:\Users\Kris\test.pdf";
        int pageNumber = 1;

        PdfExtractor extractor = new PdfExtractor();

        // Extract images from the page
        List<ExtractedImage> images = extractor.GetImagesFromPage(filePath, pageNumber);
        Console.WriteLine($"Extracted {images.Count} images from page {pageNumber}.");

        foreach (ExtractedImage image in images)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Image Data Length: {image.ImageData.Length}");
            Console.WriteLine($"Image Position: ({image.Left}, {image.Bottom})");
            Console.WriteLine($"Image Size: {image.Width}x{image.Height}");
        }

        // Extract hyperlinks from the page
        List<ExtractedHyperlink> hyperlinks = extractor.GetHyperlinksFromPage(filePath, pageNumber);
        Console.WriteLine($"Extracted {hyperlinks.Count} hyperlinks from page {pageNumber}.");

        foreach (ExtractedHyperlink hyperlink in hyperlinks)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Hyperlink Text: {hyperlink.Text}");
            Console.WriteLine($"Hyperlink URI: {hyperlink.Uri}");
            Console.WriteLine($"Hyperlink Position: ({hyperlink.Left}, {hyperlink.Bottom})");
            Console.WriteLine($"Hyperlink Size: {hyperlink.Width}x{hyperlink.Height}");
        }
    }
}
```

### Explanation

1. **PdfExtractor Class**:
   - `GetImagesFromPage`: This method opens the PDF file, retrieves the specified page, and extracts images from it. Each image is represented by an `ExtractedImage` object containing the image data and its position and size.
   - `GetHyperlinksFromPage`: This method opens the PDF file, retrieves the specified page, and extracts hyperlinks from it. Each hyperlink is represented by an `ExtractedHyperlink` object containing the text, URI, and position and size of the hyperlink.

2. **ExtractedImage and ExtractedHyperlink Classes**:
   - These classes are used to store the extracted image and hyperlink data, respectively. They include properties for the image/data, position, and size.

3. **Usage Example**:
   - The `Main` method demonstrates how to use the `PdfExtractor` class to extract images and hyperlinks from a specific page in a PDF file and print the extracted information to the console.

This solution should help you extract images and hyperlinks from a PDF page using C# and the `UglyToad.PdfPig` library.
