User: Write a Main method that demonstrates all PdfPig functionality together. Use C:\Users\Kris\test.pdf to read metadata, extract text and words, analyze layout, create a new PDF with text and shapes, and merge PDFs in a cohesive workflow.
Assistant:   To demonstrate all `PdfPig` functionality together in a cohesive workflow, you can create a `Main` method that performs the following steps:

1. **Read Metadata**: Extract metadata from the PDF document.
2. **Extract Text and Words**: Extract text and words from the PDF document.
3. **Analyze Layout**: Analyze the layout of the PDF document.
4. **Create a New PDF with Text and Shapes**: Create a new PDF document with text and shapes.
5. **Merge PDFs**: Merge multiple PDF files into a single document.

Below is a complete example demonstrating all these functionalities:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.IO;
using UglyToad.PdfPig;
using UglyToad.PdfPig.Content;
using UglyToad.PdfPig.Writer;
using UglyToad.PdfPig.Images;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        string inputFilePath = @"C:\Users\Kris\test.pdf";
        string outputFilePath = @"C:\Users\Kris\output.pdf";
        string mergedFilePath = @"C:\Users\Kris\merged.pdf";

        // Read Metadata
        PdfDocument document = PdfDocument.Open(inputFilePath);
        Console.WriteLine("Metadata:");
        Console.WriteLine($"Title: {document.Metadata.Title}");
        Console.WriteLine($"Author: {document.Metadata.Author}");
        Console.WriteLine($"Subject: {document.Metadata.Subject}");
        Console.WriteLine($"Keywords: {document.Metadata.Keywords}");

        // Extract Text and Words
        string text = document.GetText();
        Console.WriteLine("Extracted Text:");
        Console.WriteLine(text);

        List<Word> words = document.GetWords();
        Console.WriteLine("Extracted Words:");
        foreach (Word word in words)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Word: {word.Text}, Position: ({word.Bounds.Left}, {word.Bounds.Bottom})");
        }

        // Analyze Layout
        Console.WriteLine("Page Layout:");
        foreach (Page page in document.GetPages())
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Page Number: {page.Number}");
            foreach (TextBlock block in page.TextBlocks)
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"  Text Block: {block.Text}, Position: ({block.Bounds.Left}, {block.Bounds.Bottom})");
            }
        }

        // Create a New PDF with Text and Shapes
        using (MemoryStream newPdfStream = new MemoryStream())
        {
            using (PdfDocumentBuilder documentBuilder = PdfDocumentBuilder.Create(newPdfStream))
            {
                using (var page = documentBuilder.AddPage())
                {
                    PdfShapeDrawer shapeDrawer = new PdfShapeDrawer(page);

                    // Draw a rectangle
                    shapeDrawer.DrawRectangle(100, 100, 300, 300, Color.Red, 1);

                    // Draw a line
                    shapeDrawer.DrawLine(100, 100, 500, 500, Color.Black, 2);

                    // Add text
                    PdfTextDrawer textDrawer = new PdfTextDrawer(page);
                    textDrawer.SetFont("Arial", 12);
                    textDrawer.SetColor(Color.Black);
                    textDrawer.WriteText("Hello, World!", 50, 500);
                }
                documentBuilder.Save();
            }

            // Save the new PDF to a file
            File.WriteAllBytes(outputFilePath, newPdfStream.ToArray());
            Console.WriteLine($"New PDF saved to {outputFilePath}");
        }

        // Merge PDFs
        List<string> mergeFilePaths = new List<string>
        {
            inputFilePath,
            outputFilePath
        };

        PdfMerger merger = new PdfMerger();
        byte[] mergedPdf = merger.MergePdfFiles(mergeFilePaths);

        // Save the merged PDF to a file
        File.WriteAllBytes(mergedFilePath, mergedPdf);
        Console.WriteLine($"Merged PDF saved to {mergedFilePath}");
    }
}
```

### Explanation

1. **Read Metadata**:
   - The `PdfDocument.Open` method is used to open the PDF file.
   - Metadata such as title, author, subject, and keywords are extracted and printed.

2. **Extract Text and Words**:
   - The `document.GetText` method extracts the entire text from the PDF.
   - The `document.GetWords` method extracts individual words and their positions.

3. **Analyze Layout**:
   - The `document.GetPages` method retrieves all pages.
   - Each page's text blocks and their positions are printed.

4. **Create a New PDF with Text and Shapes**:
   - A new `MemoryStream` is created to hold the new PDF.
   - A `PdfDocumentBuilder` is used to create a new PDF document.
   - A rectangle, a line, and text are drawn on the new PDF.
   - The new PDF is saved to a file.

5. **Merge PDFs**:
   - A `PdfMerger` class is created to merge the input PDF and the newly created PDF.
   - The merged PDF is saved to a file.

This example demonstrates all `PdfPig` functionality together in a cohesive workflow.
